WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:07.310
I'm Alex Bui, co-director of the BD2k Center's
Coordination Center.

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.830
And welcome to the beginning of the third
section in the data science seminar series,

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:15.180
which provides an overview to computing and
different information architectures.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:20.070
So in section two week which we finished last
time, we saw how different computational tools

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:23.000
and approaches are used on different types
of biomedical data.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.029
Today, we're shifting gears somewhat and we're
learning more about the infrastructure and

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:30.160
approaches upon which these tools are going
to run.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.940
So joining us today is-- to start this overview--
is Dr. Patricia Kovatch, the founding associate

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:40.070
dean for scientific computing at the Icahn
School of Medicine at Mt.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:43.850
Sinai, where she also co-directs their Masters
of Science Biomedical Informatics.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:49.219
She's been involved in a number of NIH funded
initiatives including CHEAR, the Children's

00:00:49.219 --> 00:00:54.199
Health Exposure Analysis Resource, and the
creation of its Big Omics Data Engine.

00:00:54.199 --> 00:00:59.230
Dr. Kovatch also leads the community research
education engagement for data science project

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.839
for NIH, and prior to joining Mt.

00:01:01.839 --> 00:01:05.180
Sinai, she was director of an NSF Supercomputer
Center.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.830
Dr. Kovach, thank you for joining us today
and the floor is yours.

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:12.330
Thank you.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:17.960
And I want to start by saying-- to thanking
the organizers and saying how excited I am

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.030
to be here to do this.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:22.280
So thank you very much.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:26.181
I thought I would start out by giving a little
bit of context about Mt.

00:01:26.181 --> 00:01:33.810
Sinai because that's where I am and that gives
you some context on my view on things.

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:34.950
The Mt.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:42.480
Sinai Health System is a school of medicine
and there's also seven hospitals.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.480
There's over 18,000 babies born each year,
so it's almost like a whole village born within

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:49.080
the hospitals that we have.

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.610
So we have over eight million patients.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:56.220
And as you will see in some of my slides,
having that large patient base and having

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:02.910
the diversity of research and access to patient
data helps to drive a lot of the research

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:08.610
and things that at Sinai, which of course
is coordinated with the computing and the

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:14.180
kind of computing resources and approaches
that are necessary.

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:19.040
And so the objectives of this talk are to
provide some context on the opportunities

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.150
and challenges of that intersection of computing
and biomedical science, to provide some basic

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:28.989
knowledge on computing functionalities and
capabilities, and to provide some guidance

00:02:28.989 --> 00:02:35.709
on how to identify and develop effective,
scalable, sustainable, and reproducible computational

00:02:35.709 --> 00:02:39.189
approaches to solve the biomedical questions.

00:02:39.189 --> 00:02:45.450
To me, these are the three really important
things to help figure out-- when you think

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.659
about an overview of computing, how can you
use computing

00:02:49.659 --> 00:02:52.569
effectively, especially for biomedical science?

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:56.830
So I try to distill this down into the most
important pieces.

00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:02.549
And so to that end, we're going to talk motivation
for computation, what kinds of things people

00:03:02.549 --> 00:03:07.239
are doing with it-talk a little bit about
computing basics, so you can kind of understand

00:03:07.239 --> 00:03:10.939
a little bit about what's going on behind
the scenes.

00:03:10.939 --> 00:03:15.010
And then to me, almost the most important
thing is the process for gathering requirements

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:22.189
and really trying to understand what you want
to do so you can select the appropriate tools

00:03:22.189 --> 00:03:27.139
to use for your-- to help solve your biomedical
question.

00:03:27.139 --> 00:03:32.799
And then of course evaluation and refinement
of this computational approach so you can

00:03:32.799 --> 00:03:39.120
continue to improve and get better and expand
and solve more complex problems.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.419
And then of course what's happening behind
the scenes?

00:03:41.419 --> 00:03:47.109
What's happening coincidentally with all the
biomedical questions and how that's evolving,

00:03:47.109 --> 00:03:56.799
but also how computation and computing is
evolving as well-- so it's trying to, not

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:01.810
merge the two, but trying to bring to bear
all of these resources to specific biomedical

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:04.889
problems and questions.

00:04:04.889 --> 00:04:09.670
And so I thought I would start with the most
basic thing of all, which is why is computing

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:11.309
important to science?

00:04:11.309 --> 00:04:18.000
And some scientific problems take too long
to solve by hand or even with one computer.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.760
And so this is especially important.

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:26.440
You can think of different kinds of simulations
that are either too big, too small, too fast,

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:32.550
too slow, too dangerous, too cold, too whatever
it is, and being able to use computers for

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:36.870
these simulations is really a powerful tool.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:40.729
And of course, you can use the computers to
analyze volumes of data that would overwhelm

00:04:40.729 --> 00:04:45.860
humans processing by hand, or even just with
one computer.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:52.790
And so at the bottom of the slide here I have
a picture of a glamor shot of our supercomputer

00:04:52.790 --> 00:04:54.009
here at Mt.

00:04:54.009 --> 00:05:00.370
Sinai, that has over 13,000 compute cores,
160 terabytes of flash.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:04.789
And flash memory is like the stuff that you
have in your iPhone.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:10.190
It's not spinning disk, but we also have 20
petabytes of spinning disk, and we also have

00:05:10.190 --> 00:05:14.669
some specialized processors like GPUs.

00:05:14.669 --> 00:05:15.800
And this is a resource.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:17.639
This is part of the reason I came to Mt.

00:05:17.639 --> 00:05:23.210
Sinai, was to help partner with researchers
to understand what the computational questions

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:28.419
are that they're asking, and then help to
develop an infrastructure and expertise and

00:05:28.419 --> 00:05:32.539
resources to help solve those kind of questions
hand-in-hand.

00:05:32.539 --> 00:05:38.180
So we have over 1,200 users that are using
this resource.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:44.650
And of course because science is so collaborative
and spans multiple institutions, we have 300

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:48.599
external users on these machines as well.

00:05:48.599 --> 00:05:57.870
And again, this is just a very simple example,
but it shows you how you can have computers

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:02.260
that just do what you tell them to to really
search for things that would really be hard

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:04.689
or impossible to do by hand.

00:06:04.689 --> 00:06:08.690
Here's an example about the human genome--
3 billion bases long.

00:06:08.690 --> 00:06:14.370
This is a specific example of the TP52, the
tumor suppression protein gene, and trying

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.639
to find the genes that start with ATG.

00:06:16.639 --> 00:06:17.639
They go on triplets.

00:06:17.639 --> 00:06:19.050
They end with ACT.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:26.879
And so you can clearly see how you could harness
the power of a computer to try to find that

00:06:26.879 --> 00:06:28.409
here.

00:06:28.409 --> 00:06:34.879
And so now I'm going to give you some more
motivational, or more complex, case studies

00:06:34.879 --> 00:06:39.819
to give you some more context as to what people
use computing for and what is the role that

00:06:39.819 --> 00:06:45.300
they play, and in some sense, how a lot of
these-- what are some of the tools that are

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.250
beneath the covers to help do this science.

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:54.159
So this first example here is actually from
Marta Filizola who is a researcher at Mount

00:06:54.159 --> 00:06:55.159
Sinai.

00:06:55.159 --> 00:06:57.680
And she performs molecular dynamics simulations.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:03.460
So these are simulations where you're trying
to understand how proteins interact with each

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:04.460
other.

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:08.250
And if we can understand how to do that, then
you can develop, let's say, a drug or you

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:14.350
can develop something that can interrupt that
process that may be creating disease or developing

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:15.350
disease.

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.949
So this is the kind of simulations that she
does.

00:07:17.949 --> 00:07:24.000
And she's doing it specifically around a drug
designed for nonaddictive painkillers.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.280
And so beneath the scenes, she uses this Gromacs
code, and there's lots of molecular dynamics

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:36.750
codes or community codes that anyone can download
and use and modify specifically for your own

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:37.750
purposes.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:42.789
But Gromacs, the code itself, is 1.3 million
lines of c-code.

00:07:42.789 --> 00:07:45.150
And she runs that on our supercomputer here.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:55.219
So she'll takeover-- use thousands of processors
and take over it to help understand how these

00:07:55.219 --> 00:07:58.340
proteins are interacting.

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:03.830
Another example-- another computing example--
is with neurosurgery simulation.

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:10.990
So you can think of flight simulators and
all these other things that help clinicians--

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:17.259
sorry-- that help pilots practice flying a
plane and simulating different conditions

00:08:17.259 --> 00:08:18.599
where things can go wrong.

00:08:18.599 --> 00:08:22.789
And so this kind of capability is not a widespread
practice.

00:08:22.789 --> 00:08:26.030
It's not a widespread practice for neurosurgery.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:30.680
And so it seems obvious that this would be
a really useful tool.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:38.120
It would help residents know if they would
actually be good surgeons and to help assess

00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:43.510
the skills of potential surgeons but also
existing surgeons.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:49.060
But you could also imagine where you could
do a patient specific practice of a surgery

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.570
before it happens.

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:57.610
And that could reduce the amount of time in
the waiting room-- sorry-- the time in the

00:08:57.610 --> 00:08:58.610
operating room.

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:04.360
And it could reduce complications, and be
less stress, less cost, all of these things.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:09.350
And so here, the two images that you're looking
at- the top image-- is an actual image from

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:11.310
a neurosurgery.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.130
And the bottom one is from a basic simulator.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:19.330
And you can see the difference about how it's
just not really that realistic.

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:24.750
And some of the not realistic part is about
you want to be able to simulate if you hit

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:30.260
a vein, that the vein breaks, and all of these
kinds of things.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:36.870
And the brain is like wet tissue paper or
so I'm told, from working with the neurosurgeons.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:46.560
And so how do you develop a tissue deformation
model that is going to be accurate and gentle

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:52.170
enough and specific enough to actually simulate
what it actually feels like?

00:09:52.170 --> 00:09:55.930
And some of that's in the haptic feedback
devices and them being sensitive enough to

00:09:55.930 --> 00:09:56.930
do it.

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:00.880
Some of it is in, as I was mentioning, the
different tissue deformation models.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:06.779
Some of it is in the blood flow models that
maybe can be replicated with finite element

00:10:06.779 --> 00:10:07.779
models.

00:10:07.779 --> 00:10:12.670
So there's things from other fields that can
be brought to bear on these kinds of problems.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:20.290
So I mentioned these finite element models
to mirror how the blood flows through the

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:21.290
body.

00:10:21.290 --> 00:10:26.230
And so those models are similar to laminar
flow models on airplanes and in other fields.

00:10:26.230 --> 00:10:32.050
So to me, one of the important things is that--
how can we leverage all of this expertise

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:36.700
from these other fields and bring them to
bear on biomedical problems?

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:45.399
So on this slide, you can see there's lots
of different models and things, and complexities.

00:10:45.399 --> 00:10:51.589
We're very far away from being able to do
this realistically.

00:10:51.589 --> 00:10:56.100
And yet this is obviously a very important
thing to do.

00:10:56.100 --> 00:11:02.089
And along that same road, there's also things
like-- you can imagine practically every organ

00:11:02.089 --> 00:11:04.540
or the whole body simulation.

00:11:04.540 --> 00:11:09.899
But even if you think about the heart, we
want to be able to do things like understand--

00:11:09.899 --> 00:11:14.260
you go back to the molecular dynamics simulations--
so understand how the proteins are working,

00:11:14.260 --> 00:11:18.380
understand how the whole cell is operating,
understand how all those cells combined together

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:23.460
to make an organ, and then also interact with
the vascular system and everything else.

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:31.280
So you can see how these multi-skilled models
are very important, I think, to the future

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:32.360
of health care.

00:11:32.360 --> 00:11:36.880
Because what you want to be able to do is
simulate this stuff in less than real time.

00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:42.270
So what you want to be able to do is simulate
heartbeats and all these things in less than

00:11:42.270 --> 00:11:47.589
real time so you can introduce different therapeutics,
and see how long it would take for this drug

00:11:47.589 --> 00:11:52.019
to react, or will that drug actually help
this patient.

00:11:52.019 --> 00:11:58.339
And like I said, make us all patient specific--
because every patient, there's a canonical

00:11:58.339 --> 00:11:59.339
patient.

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:01.880
But who does that really match?

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:05.980
So how can we build all these models to be
able to do this?

00:12:05.980 --> 00:12:10.250
And obviously, like I said before, we are
really far away from doing this.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:14.300
But I believe that this is really the future
of being able to do this stuff.

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:21.880
And on this slide, it shows you-- you know
the reasons why to do this and some of the

00:12:21.880 --> 00:12:28.230
complex computational things that we need
to do to get there.

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:33.899
And another example is there's a researcher,
Joseph Buxbaum, at Mt.

00:12:33.899 --> 00:12:37.620
Sinai who's working on building the world's
largest autism database.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:44.940
So my understanding is there's about 500 such
genes that we think are related to autism,

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:51.330
and so in some sense, some of the approaches
to solving some of these problems are getting

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:55.709
as much data as possible-- so getting as many
people, and their brothers and sisters and

00:12:55.709 --> 00:13:01.731
mothers and sons and all this, so that you
can start to find the patterns in the data--

00:13:01.731 --> 00:13:04.080
to locate what these other genes might be.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:08.779
And again, once you find those genes, then
you can use some of those molecular dynamics

00:13:08.779 --> 00:13:15.519
simulations and other things to help develop
different therapeutics that could help these

00:13:15.519 --> 00:13:16.519
patients.

00:13:16.519 --> 00:13:26.220
So again, you can see how this is all related
and important.

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:28.639
So some of the stuff uses a lot of computation.

00:13:28.639 --> 00:13:30.380
It loses a lot of storage.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:35.450
This project specifically is using about two
petabytes of storage, and part of it's to

00:13:35.450 --> 00:13:40.420
store all the genetic stuff and all the secondary
and tertiary data and all of this stuff.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:46.060
So you come up with-- and I guess I'll say
a petabyte is 1,000 terabytes and a terabyte

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:50.420
is 1,000 gigabytes, and a gigabyte is 1,000
megabytes.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:55.079
So like on your laptop or something, you probably
have a couple hundred gigabytes.

00:13:55.079 --> 00:14:02.899
So this shows you how many orders of magnitude
more this data is and, like I said, trying

00:14:02.899 --> 00:14:08.670
to take all of this data and make some sense
of it is a real project.

00:14:08.670 --> 00:14:13.930
And along that path, here's an example of
around personalized cancer therapies, about

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:18.220
again, trying to analyze patient specific
mutations so you can find the appropriate

00:14:18.220 --> 00:14:21.160
therapy for each individual patient.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:23.850
And you can look at the different cancers.

00:14:23.850 --> 00:14:30.340
We now know that cancer is a-- each tumor
has its own genetic makeup and all of these

00:14:30.340 --> 00:14:31.340
things.

00:14:31.340 --> 00:14:38.899
So we're running-- again, this is another
application that uses a lot of computing and

00:14:38.899 --> 00:14:45.149
using the language languages, R, Perl, Python,
C, C++ to do this, and again using it on our

00:14:45.149 --> 00:14:49.279
supercomputer that we have here at Sinai.

00:14:49.279 --> 00:14:54.360
And of course, imaging, as you can imagine,
the images also take a lot of space.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:59.800
And there is a lot of complexity here as well
in terms of being able to identify tumors.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:07.959
And so you can see at the top of this slide
here the-- maybe I should say unfortunate

00:15:07.959 --> 00:15:18.380
accuracy in terms of reading images.

00:15:18.380 --> 00:15:21.971
And the reference to this is on the paper
at the bottom, and there's other ones that

00:15:21.971 --> 00:15:23.670
are similar as well.

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:29.290
And so how can we get computers to automatically
recognize tumors?

00:15:29.290 --> 00:15:34.920
There's a project that I worked with some
folks on where tumors that were malignant

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:37.379
had a fractal like pattern on the outside
of it.

00:15:37.379 --> 00:15:42.829
And so working with different applied mathematicians
to help be able to recognize that automatically

00:15:42.829 --> 00:15:49.680
and maybe recognize it a lot sooner than what
you could see with the human eye would be

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.889
obviously very unhelpful to lots of people.

00:15:51.889 --> 00:15:58.879
There's lots of false positives and all of
these things that help create stress.

00:15:58.879 --> 00:16:03.420
And you can see from this ultrasound example
here about how many images there are, just

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:04.699
looping through all these images.

00:16:04.699 --> 00:16:12.020
If there was a backup system that could help
the radiologist look and see things that they

00:16:12.020 --> 00:16:17.639
might accidentally miss, you can see how this
would be really useful.

00:16:17.639 --> 00:16:23.050
And you can also see-- this next slide gives
you some order of magnitude on that amount

00:16:23.050 --> 00:16:26.110
of exams and the amount of images that we
have.

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:34.129
And so the thing is that with this quantity
of data, can we train computers to automatically

00:16:34.129 --> 00:16:42.680
recognize these images or to recognize specific
features in these images?

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:47.940
And maybe by looking as early as possible
in someone's history or everyone's history

00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:54.230
to see something developing-- but also again,
trying to find the patterns between all of

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:56.350
these images.

00:16:56.350 --> 00:17:00.019
And so how can we do this?

00:17:00.019 --> 00:17:02.100
So you can see.

00:17:02.100 --> 00:17:09.780
And all of this goes into this overall vision
for precision medicine, where you can take

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:14.990
all of these different-- whether it's Twitter
feeds or information about weather and climate

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:21.750
and all of these things-- and you combine
into developing these predictive models of

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:27.589
disease.

00:17:27.589 --> 00:17:31.169
And obviously, this is a difficult problem
because all of this data is different.

00:17:31.169 --> 00:17:37.080
How do you figure out which data is important,
which data is not important, which ones--

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:39.730
it
seems like things are related but are they

00:17:39.730 --> 00:17:42.270
actually related?

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:46.039
Correlation is not the same as causation,
and all of these concepts.

00:17:46.039 --> 00:17:51.909
So you can see how this is a pretty big problem
in general, but also a pretty good computational

00:17:51.909 --> 00:17:54.390
and data problem too.

00:17:54.390 --> 00:18:03.090
And as I've said, you can see here, this is
an example specific to proteins.

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:07.090
The problems are so complex that you really
need a team of researchers working together

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:12.350
and researchers from all different domains,
whether it's engineering, or computer science,

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:17.040
or applied math, to work on a single problem
together.

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:20.760
So there's of course lots of examples of people
doing this.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:27.679
I could argue that in every single example
that I've just shown, obviously there were

00:18:27.679 --> 00:18:33.460
computer scientists, computational scientists,
lots of different people involved to help

00:18:33.460 --> 00:18:39.340
make these models able to run and to help
work towards solving these biomedical questions.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:44.850
So I thought I'd spend a little bit on the
computing basics to just give you some sense

00:18:44.850 --> 00:18:48.570
of how-- a little bit about how computers
work.

00:18:48.570 --> 00:18:56.970
So we think in human language like English,
but computers operate by some kind of machine

00:18:56.970 --> 00:18:57.970
code.

00:18:57.970 --> 00:19:03.220
And so we think in English or some other language,
but then we have to translate that into some

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:07.870
kind of computer language that ends up getting
translated to machine code so the computers

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:10.150
can do the operations on it.

00:19:10.150 --> 00:19:14.330
And so I wanted to make the point here that
there's a whole bunch of different languages.

00:19:14.330 --> 00:19:22.610
And I'm sure you all have heard about lots
of languages on this graph here, but also

00:19:22.610 --> 00:19:24.370
many other languages.

00:19:24.370 --> 00:19:28.559
And I think the main thing that I want to
get across here is that different languages

00:19:28.559 --> 00:19:34.410
work in different ways and are good for different
kinds of computational problems.

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:36.679
And some of them may have better performance.

00:19:36.679 --> 00:19:45.159
Some of them may have libraries or other pieces
in here that make it easy to do and simple

00:19:45.159 --> 00:19:47.470
to do the kinds of calculations you want to
do.

00:19:47.470 --> 00:19:53.761
And so you kind of have to-- they're all good
first something and you have to figure out

00:19:53.761 --> 00:19:57.490
if they're good for what you want to do.

00:19:57.490 --> 00:20:07.120
And so in this diagram, all modern computers,
or virtually all modern computers today use

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:11.700
this architecture, the Von Neumann architecture.

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:16.539
So you would write a program that instructs
this control unit to load your program from

00:20:16.539 --> 00:20:22.919
disk to memory, and then the control unit
executes the instructions in the program,

00:20:22.919 --> 00:20:23.919
holding your data in memory.

00:20:23.919 --> 00:20:32.460
And then it writes something out, and then
the ALU performs the calculations on it.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:37.870
And so the reason why this is important is
because the way to-

00:20:37.870 --> 00:20:39.010
[INAUDIBLE]
I'm sorry?

00:20:39.010 --> 00:20:45.250
So the reason this is important is because
each of the languages-- how do I say-- the

00:20:45.250 --> 00:20:55.980
way each of the languages use-- the way that
your data and your program-- the way your

00:20:55.980 --> 00:21:05.890
program will process the data and how efficient
it is at doing that is dependent on this computer

00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:08.100
architecture.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:13.260
And so you can see here at the bottom, this
is latency numbers every programmer should

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:14.260
know.

00:21:14.260 --> 00:21:22.169
So this shows you how long it takes to read
a program or read data from disk.

00:21:22.169 --> 00:21:27.010
And you can see down here how many orders
of magnitude more it is to read a program

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:28.010
from disk.

00:21:28.010 --> 00:21:32.000
And all of this data has to get read from
disk and brought into memory to be operated

00:21:32.000 --> 00:21:35.450
on.

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:40.539
And like I said, different programming languages
and different other utilities are more efficient

00:21:40.539 --> 00:21:45.400
at-- you can't change the physical hardware
that's underlying all this, but some of the

00:21:45.400 --> 00:21:52.909
languages and some of the techniques can help
speed this up and-- even within C and the

00:21:52.909 --> 00:21:57.360
different languages, there's different constructs
in the different languages that can operate

00:21:57.360 --> 00:21:58.880
on data more smartly.

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:05.820
And so that ends up being a big deal because
all of us sit around and we can't figure out

00:22:05.820 --> 00:22:08.320
why some web page won't load or whatever it
is.

00:22:08.320 --> 00:22:13.279
We're waiting forever for something to happen,
and a lot of is because of how the programs

00:22:13.279 --> 00:22:14.279
were written.

00:22:14.279 --> 00:22:20.280
So in fact, I have a little example on this
later so I'll get to that.

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:24.460
And what I wanted to show in this slide is
a little bit of-- so in the last slide, I

00:22:24.460 --> 00:22:27.360
was showing you how an individual computer
works.

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:34.200
But then these individual computers get banded
together to provide much larger computing

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:35.919
services.

00:22:35.919 --> 00:22:39.351
And so this is whether it's at Amazon or it's
at Mt.

00:22:39.351 --> 00:22:45.929
Sinai or it's virtually anywhere, like Wal-mart
or something.

00:22:45.929 --> 00:22:50.080
And so it's banding together all these nodes
together with some kind of network.

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:54.460
In the case of high performance computing,
it would be banded together with a high performance

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:56.170
low latency network.

00:22:56.170 --> 00:22:58.159
But there is storage that's connected.

00:22:58.159 --> 00:23:00.240
There is different web services.

00:23:00.240 --> 00:23:01.240
There's connection to the internet.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:03.930
And all of these things band together.

00:23:03.930 --> 00:23:11.390
And like I said, Amazon has-- everyone has
virtually the same kind of hardware, infrastructure,

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:12.390
behind.

00:23:12.390 --> 00:23:16.410
But it's the different software that you put
on it that can make it operate in different

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:23.520
ways and perform better or more usefully for
whatever your application is that you're doing.

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:31.539
And so here's an example of a genomics-- the
Broad's best practices tool kit, and some

00:23:31.539 --> 00:23:34.549
of the different steps that are involved with
that.

00:23:34.549 --> 00:23:37.650
So these different steps-- some pieces of
this are run on the nervous.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:43.460
Some pieces of this are run-- like the very
first step here that's run right after the

00:23:43.460 --> 00:23:46.040
data comes off the aluminum sequencer.

00:23:46.040 --> 00:23:49.370
Some of the stuff, like the end step that
the analysis dashboard-- is running on someone's

00:23:49.370 --> 00:23:50.910
laptop.

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:58.810
And I'm just trying to show the big picture
on how all of this stuff kind of fits together.

00:23:58.810 --> 00:24:06.140
And so all of these different steps launch
different processes and need to be monitored

00:24:06.140 --> 00:24:11.520
and need to be checked and happen in a sequential
way.

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:14.320
And the data needs to be moved and checked
and all of these things.

00:24:14.320 --> 00:24:19.580
And you can see on the right-hand part of
the slide here that these are some of this

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:22.299
software and some of the languages that were
used.

00:24:22.299 --> 00:24:26.649
And as you can see, there's a whole diversity
of languages that were used, and it's because

00:24:26.649 --> 00:24:32.640
again, some languages are better for doing
certain kinds of tasks than other languages.

00:24:32.640 --> 00:24:37.260
So to me, a lot of it is trying to figure
out what the right tools are for what you

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:38.679
want to do.

00:24:38.679 --> 00:24:43.350
And so this next slide shows-- so in some
sense, from the system's perspective-- so

00:24:43.350 --> 00:24:47.659
you have the infrastructure that's there,
then you have the application that's running

00:24:47.659 --> 00:24:52.850
on top of this infrastructure, even if it's
a multi-stage workflow.

00:24:52.850 --> 00:25:00.620
And then you have, what is that workflow doing
and what's happening back on the system?

00:25:00.620 --> 00:25:07.200
And so this workflow that you just saw, it
will submit 10,000 to 20,000 jobs at one time,

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:09.470
and this is to work on one genome.

00:25:09.470 --> 00:25:14.299
And so actually each of these jobs only run
for a few minutes at a time, but then there's

00:25:14.299 --> 00:25:18.830
other workflow job managers that are appalling
to make sure that the jobs are really completing

00:25:18.830 --> 00:25:22.490
and the next step doesn't move on until the
last step was done.

00:25:22.490 --> 00:25:31.500
And again, you can see how, in some sense,
how quickly complex it gets where you have--

00:25:31.500 --> 00:25:36.720
I will say only a 200 gigabyte directory,
but it will have a million files in it and

00:25:36.720 --> 00:25:37.990
10,000 folders.

00:25:37.990 --> 00:25:41.891
And again, to me, this goes into some of this
stuff-- how do you manage your data?

00:25:41.891 --> 00:25:47.299
And how can all of this stuff-how can you
get your workflow and get your science done

00:25:47.299 --> 00:25:49.720
in an optimized way?

00:25:49.720 --> 00:25:55.330
And so we actually did-- if anyone is interested
in this-- we actually looked at our usage

00:25:55.330 --> 00:26:02.700
and our workflow here, and we optimized the
system specifically to work for this workflow

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:04.620
so they could be efficient.

00:26:04.620 --> 00:26:11.580
And the idea here being is that if you can
get more genome sequenced, if you can get

00:26:11.580 --> 00:26:15.610
more work done, you can get more science done.

00:26:15.610 --> 00:26:22.309
And so having a pipeline and having an infrastructure--
and that's the application, that's also the

00:26:22.309 --> 00:26:28.240
hardware and the software underneath it--
that works efficiently, you can really get

00:26:28.240 --> 00:26:33.340
more science done, which having that throughput
is a really important thing.

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:39.390
So that's how we ended up with so much flash
storage because of all of the tiny files that

00:26:39.390 --> 00:26:40.940
were being created.

00:26:40.940 --> 00:26:46.220
And instead of them being written to disk,
which is slow because a disk is a mechanical

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:51.320
device, they're just being written to these
mini memories like in your iPhone.

00:26:51.320 --> 00:26:56.809
And so that makes the I/O for these kinds
of processes much, much, much faster.

00:26:56.809 --> 00:26:59.169
And I'll just contrast it.

00:26:59.169 --> 00:27:04.460
This is very different than other workflows,
like let's say an astrophysics simulation,

00:27:04.460 --> 00:27:10.659
where the simulation-- let's say the creation
of the universe-- and you're writing out these

00:27:10.659 --> 00:27:12.450
large sequential files.

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:18.299
And so in some sense, the underlying software
can easily predict which file it's going to

00:27:18.299 --> 00:27:21.279
need next or which portion of disk it's going
to need next.

00:27:21.279 --> 00:27:29.409
And so you can have a really efficient pipeline
of data being read into memory-- being

00:27:29.409 --> 00:27:34.809
moved into memory and read by the processor
so you can get efficient computation.

00:27:34.809 --> 00:27:39.559
But with the genomic workflows, you kind of
have lots of tiny files in all different sorts

00:27:39.559 --> 00:27:43.730
of places, and these files are very small.

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:49.299
So when they're so small like that, they don't
even fit into what we call blocks on the disk.

00:27:49.299 --> 00:27:54.960
So the disk is arranged into many different
pieces.

00:27:54.960 --> 00:28:01.710
It has many different slots that it can put
data into.

00:28:01.710 --> 00:28:06.419
So by not even making it to this mechanical
thing with the disk head moving around and

00:28:06.419 --> 00:28:12.350
all this stuff trying to do all this searching
to find your data-- and this is why the latency

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:18.760
numbers are so high for writing to disk that
we're on the Von Neumann slide-- this is all

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:22.820
much more efficient if it can just be written
to flash storage or stay in memory.

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:28.289
So a lot of the genomic data is too big to
store everything in memory and do the computations

00:28:28.289 --> 00:28:41.850
on it there, so that's why having some space
that's certainly way more faster than old

00:28:41.850 --> 00:28:45.530
style disk, you can see how this can speed
the computation and how you can actually get

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:47.460
more science done.

00:28:47.460 --> 00:28:49.910
So this is really important.

00:28:49.910 --> 00:28:57.130
And so I'm going to talk now about developing
an effective computational approach and putting

00:28:57.130 --> 00:29:02.140
all these pieces, so that you too can help
develop your own workflow.

00:29:02.140 --> 00:29:09.350
So to me, the very first thing that's the
most important of all practically is thinking

00:29:09.350 --> 00:29:12.830
about what your biomedical question is.

00:29:12.830 --> 00:29:17.669
And then you can think about how computing
can help it.

00:29:17.669 --> 00:29:19.799
What problem are you trying to solve?

00:29:19.799 --> 00:29:24.539
Often I talk to people who say, oh, I want
to use machine learning on this and this and

00:29:24.539 --> 00:29:27.950
this, and I want to use GPUs and this.

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:34.710
But they're already at the solution and they
haven't really thought through what are they

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:40.720
really trying to do, because that may or may
not be the right tool for that problem.

00:29:40.720 --> 00:29:45.730
So to me, it's really important to think about
this and really try to understand.

00:29:45.730 --> 00:29:50.571
And of course, you can think about how things
may change over time, because now, maybe I

00:29:50.571 --> 00:29:52.909
know I'm going to have to process a certain
amount of data.

00:29:52.909 --> 00:29:56.970
But a year from now, I'm expecting that to
be two times as much.

00:29:56.970 --> 00:30:02.350
And is whatever I'm developing or whatever
other software I'm adopting from someone else--

00:30:02.350 --> 00:30:05.549
can it actually scale to that much?

00:30:05.549 --> 00:30:11.830
Or am I going to be introducing other complex
data that is not going to fit into this program

00:30:11.830 --> 00:30:14.480
that either I wrote or that I adopted from
someone else?

00:30:14.480 --> 00:30:19.950
So you have to think, what is your timeline
on this and what you know what problem are

00:30:19.950 --> 00:30:21.010
you trying to solve?

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:27.580
But a lot of it comes into when do you have
to do this by and what all do you need?

00:30:27.580 --> 00:30:33.590
And you can see on here, there's the functional
requirements, in some sense, which is the

00:30:33.590 --> 00:30:36.990
discrete things that you want the computer
to do for you.

00:30:36.990 --> 00:30:42.559
But then there's the other things, which are
nonfunctional, which are things like is there

00:30:42.559 --> 00:30:45.390
a certain reliability or maintainability?

00:30:45.390 --> 00:30:48.419
Is this a code you know you're somewhere and
you're going to be somewhere else next year,

00:30:48.419 --> 00:30:53.130
and you know they have different kinds of
processors and where you are now, you have

00:30:53.130 --> 00:30:56.550
Intel processors and they have AMD or power
processors.

00:30:56.550 --> 00:31:02.150
And so maybe you want to pick a computing
language they can port to those other things

00:31:02.150 --> 00:31:04.380
without having to rebuild all your code.

00:31:04.380 --> 00:31:08.390
So there's lots of things to think about here.

00:31:08.390 --> 00:31:17.980
And then of course it's important to think
about how do you know that it'll be successful?

00:31:17.980 --> 00:31:19.540
What are your metrics for success?

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:24.230
How do you know that this approach-- if the
approach will be solving your scientific question

00:31:24.230 --> 00:31:30.309
and how would you evaluate its effectiveness?

00:31:30.309 --> 00:31:31.870
There's lots of ways to get to the store.

00:31:31.870 --> 00:31:38.279
It could be like a hot air balloon or a Cadillac
or a bike or walking and there's lots of ways.

00:31:38.279 --> 00:31:42.100
But what do you consider effective and what
are your constraints?

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:43.620
Is it time?

00:31:43.620 --> 00:31:44.620
Is it money?

00:31:44.620 --> 00:31:48.670
All of these things go into thinking about
how to do this.

00:31:48.670 --> 00:31:54.179
And then of course it's planning it out and
thinking about where the data is going to

00:31:54.179 --> 00:31:55.179
and from.

00:31:55.179 --> 00:32:02.450
So on the bottom here is an example from Arthur
Goldberg at Mt Sinai who-- he's doing whole

00:32:02.450 --> 00:32:03.799
cell modeling.

00:32:03.799 --> 00:32:11.610
And so you can see some of the high level
steps in their process to do this.

00:32:11.610 --> 00:32:15.400
And every single one of these steps, there's
like lots of subsets of course.

00:32:15.400 --> 00:32:21.730
There's another pipeline to figure out-- developing
the models and developing a parallel simulation--

00:32:21.730 --> 00:32:22.730
so all these things.

00:32:22.730 --> 00:32:27.350
So it's important to think through what these
things are and where you want the data going

00:32:27.350 --> 00:32:29.780
to and from.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:34.570
And I've mentioned this already because to
me this is extremely important too.

00:32:34.570 --> 00:32:39.480
It's trying to see what other approaches already
exist.

00:32:39.480 --> 00:32:45.929
And a lot of it is that there's so many other
investments that NIH and NSF and DOE have

00:32:45.929 --> 00:32:46.929
already made.

00:32:46.929 --> 00:32:51.029
So there's lots of tools that are already
out there.

00:32:51.029 --> 00:32:58.460
And one thing is that I see a lot is that--
of course there's lots of clever people that

00:32:58.460 --> 00:33:02.280
want to redo all this or reinvent the wheel.

00:33:02.280 --> 00:33:07.110
But often you can find something that is already
existing.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:13.230
And I always try to help people to put their
talents into developing something new and

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:16.660
making it scalable and solving a problem that
hasn't been solved already.

00:33:16.660 --> 00:33:25.290
So you have to figure out if it makes sense
of course, but trying to figure out what's

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:29.130
the right approach-- so some of this is around
the computational platform that you might

00:33:29.130 --> 00:33:32.710
want to use, whether it's high performance
computing or the cloud or some of these other

00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:33.710
things.

00:33:33.710 --> 00:33:35.799
How are you going to judge effectiveness?

00:33:35.799 --> 00:33:37.139
Is it time of solution?

00:33:37.139 --> 00:33:39.159
Is it ease of use?

00:33:39.159 --> 00:33:43.909
There's all different kinds of things that
are-- all different kinds of characteristics

00:33:43.909 --> 00:33:44.909
that are important.

00:33:44.909 --> 00:33:48.880
And it depends on what you're doing to figure
out which ones of these are the most important

00:33:48.880 --> 00:33:53.340
and how you can design your design around
this.

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:58.710
But to me, the first thing I always do is
see what other people are doing, especially

00:33:58.710 --> 00:34:01.659
people at other institutions and working on
similar questions.

00:34:01.659 --> 00:34:08.130
Because chances are they've already done a
lot.

00:34:08.130 --> 00:34:11.040
And so it's good to know what that is.

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:15.170
And part of this effective approach, in addition
to being able to leverage what other people

00:34:15.170 --> 00:34:19.410
have done, is again,
this is on the programming languages.

00:34:19.410 --> 00:34:26.310
So you can see here, courtesy of Hyung Min
Cho who works at Mount Sinai and is a competition

00:34:26.310 --> 00:34:33.200
scientist-- he multiplied two matrices and
he did it in three different ways.

00:34:33.200 --> 00:34:38.530
And you can seen several orders of magnitude
speed up between these.

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:46.450
And the first one was in Python with 6,400
seconds, like an implementation in C, a naive

00:34:46.450 --> 00:34:48.680
one-- 25 seconds.

00:34:48.680 --> 00:34:55.740
But if he uses BLAS which is a numerical library
that is available on the web and open source

00:34:55.740 --> 00:34:59.640
and easy to use, it gets it down to one second.

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:04.430
And again, you can quickly see, depending
on how many times you have to do a matrix

00:35:04.430 --> 00:35:10.020
computation like this, you can quickly see
how your runtime of your program can be drastically

00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:15.890
different depending on a, which kind of language
you use, but b, if you leverage the work of

00:35:15.890 --> 00:35:22.050
others who have already spent a lot of time
optimizing and improving this stuff.

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:26.830
And the faster runtime, the more jobs you
can run and the more science you can get done.

00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:31.030
So this is really important.

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:33.820
So now you're building together your pipeline.

00:35:33.820 --> 00:35:36.710
You're starting to identify what these different
components are.

00:35:36.710 --> 00:35:45.190
And of course it's important to test each
component so you know how it's working is

00:35:45.190 --> 00:35:48.470
how-- if it's working the way it should be
working.

00:35:48.470 --> 00:35:53.010
And I think it's also important to think about
whether the results make sense.

00:35:53.010 --> 00:36:00.740
So I mean I've worked at some-there's lots
of things that can go wrong here and maybe

00:36:00.740 --> 00:36:02.430
some things that are unexpected.

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:09.600
So there is a supercomputer that I worked
on-- I don't know-- maybe 15 years ago now.

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:16.230
And where the bandwidth, low latency interconnect
that was connecting the nodes together was--

00:36:16.230 --> 00:36:22.750
in certain conditions, it was giving out spurious
answers.

00:36:22.750 --> 00:36:30.730
And in some sense, some of you may remember
the Intel Pentium bug from, I think, 2004.

00:36:30.730 --> 00:36:35.160
So even the computers can give you wrong answers
and work in unexpected ways.

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:39.380
And sometimes it's the hardware, sometimes
it's the software.

00:36:39.380 --> 00:36:46.400
And back to my initial example about the high
performance interconnect that didn't work

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:53.630
properly-- there were thousands of users on
that system and only one reported the error

00:36:53.630 --> 00:36:54.630
to me.

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:59.090
And it only happened intermittently and all
these things so it was really complicated

00:36:59.090 --> 00:37:00.390
to debug.

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:06.770
But it makes me wonder about all of that other
science that was being done on that machine,

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:09.720
and how many other people were seeing this.

00:37:09.720 --> 00:37:14.820
And we worked for a long time to get it reproducible,
to get the bug to reproduce so that we could

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:15.880
solve the problem.

00:37:15.880 --> 00:37:19.370
And it ended up being a problem with the chip
design itself.

00:37:19.370 --> 00:37:27.160
But when you think about all that science
and all these people running and some percentage

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:30.470
of them were definitely not getting the right
answers, and did anyone even notice?

00:37:30.470 --> 00:37:33.570
Because like I said, only one person reported
that to me.

00:37:33.570 --> 00:37:36.640
So I don't know.

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:42.380
That's why I think looking at the answers
and really thinking about them-- even just

00:37:42.380 --> 00:37:43.380
saying, does this make sense?

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:47.630
And you can compare it to maybe something
in the lab or something what other people

00:37:47.630 --> 00:37:52.760
have done, or maybe you compare to answers
somewhere else.

00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:58.480
Or there's just lots of-- you can see how
clearly this is really important.

00:37:58.480 --> 00:37:59.690
OK.

00:37:59.690 --> 00:38:02.120
Let's see.

00:38:02.120 --> 00:38:03.340
OK.

00:38:03.340 --> 00:38:07.890
So next step is-- and again, I'm already talking
about this a little bit, about the validation

00:38:07.890 --> 00:38:09.520
and verifying the results.

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:13.920
So does your workflow solve what you wanted
it to do?

00:38:13.920 --> 00:38:18.610
Do your results make sense?

00:38:18.610 --> 00:38:20.840
And then there's things of making it more
robust.

00:38:20.840 --> 00:38:24.500
So can you automate all or part of the workflow?

00:38:24.500 --> 00:38:25.630
Does it make sense to?

00:38:25.630 --> 00:38:29.990
Can you monitor it so you know that things
are failing?

00:38:29.990 --> 00:38:35.670
You could see, maybe in the genomics example,
where you could-- especially when things are

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:41.220
supposed to happen in a sequential way-- you
want to make sure that the next step isn't

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:44.690
starting until the last step is done, and
in some sense you know that the last step

00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:46.370
has some kind of good answer.

00:38:46.370 --> 00:38:51.610
So is there some way when the answer comes
or when you get some kind of results to even

00:38:51.610 --> 00:38:55.720
check that against some kind-- do a little
bit of parameter checking so you know that,

00:38:55.720 --> 00:38:58.640
yeah, that's OK, now let's move on to the
next step.

00:38:58.640 --> 00:39:06.590
So these things are important and important
to check for.

00:39:06.590 --> 00:39:11.820
And then of course moving onto evaluating
the confrontational approach-- so how does

00:39:11.820 --> 00:39:14.580
it perform according to your predefined metrics?

00:39:14.580 --> 00:39:17.830
Would it work better on a different platform?

00:39:17.830 --> 00:39:26.880
And the genomics example that I showed earlier
about the GATK pipeline-- we benchmarked the

00:39:26.880 --> 00:39:31.530
parts of the genomic pipeline that take the
most computation-- we benchmarked them on

00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:35.080
a variety of processors.

00:39:35.080 --> 00:39:39.130
Because each processor, motherboard, and all
this, are designed slightly differently and

00:39:39.130 --> 00:39:44.690
they have different memory bandwidth and different
cache sizes and different I/O bandwidth.

00:39:44.690 --> 00:39:51.050
And so all of these things being different
can make a really big difference on how well

00:39:51.050 --> 00:39:54.500
your program runs and again how much science
you can get done.

00:39:54.500 --> 00:39:59.850
So really understanding that I think is really
important.

00:39:59.850 --> 00:40:07.800
And also being able to have a baseline for
how your workflow works will help you-- that's

00:40:07.800 --> 00:40:12.060
also another way that when something is off.

00:40:12.060 --> 00:40:14.600
And it could be something off with the system.

00:40:14.600 --> 00:40:16.120
Maybe there's a file system problem.

00:40:16.120 --> 00:40:17.790
Maybe there's some other problem.

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:23.420
But unless you know how long it normally takes
or what the results are, it's hard to know

00:40:23.420 --> 00:40:28.720
when something is off.

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:31.860
It's also good to have a test harness or some
kind of regression test.

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:36.340
So let's say you're relying on a whole bunch
of numerical libraries or you're relying on

00:40:36.340 --> 00:40:39.000
other things, other kinds of software.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:43.970
Well again, all this software, when it changes,
that could have an inadvertent change in your

00:40:43.970 --> 00:40:45.630
code too and in your results.

00:40:45.630 --> 00:40:51.230
So understanding that baseline and running
some basic tests that you know you're still

00:40:51.230 --> 00:40:56.890
getting the same results every time, even
after software changes, even if it's a system

00:40:56.890 --> 00:41:00.950
software change, is really important to do.

00:41:00.950 --> 00:41:01.950
OK.

00:41:01.950 --> 00:41:09.710
And then of course it's important to disseminate
your results, share them so that other people

00:41:09.710 --> 00:41:13.440
can leverage the things that you've done.

00:41:13.440 --> 00:41:18.690
And this goes along with creating this plan
for the future and sustainability.

00:41:18.690 --> 00:41:23.440
You put together some pipeline and it takes
an enormous amount of effort.

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:28.550
And software can live forever, sometimes inadvertently.

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:33.830
And it's almost like the costs start when
you think you're finished writing it.

00:41:33.830 --> 00:41:43.970
So this is a really important thing to figure
out moving forward.

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:49.660
So there's a whole bunch of best practices
that you can use as part of developing all

00:41:49.660 --> 00:41:52.310
of these, as you develop your requirements.

00:41:52.310 --> 00:41:56.520
So things like having the data management
plan-- where are you going to store your data?

00:41:56.520 --> 00:41:57.560
Who's going to have access?

00:41:57.560 --> 00:41:58.950
How are you going to share it?

00:41:58.950 --> 00:42:01.340
How can you recover from a disaster?

00:42:01.340 --> 00:42:03.440
And that's not even just making backups.

00:42:03.440 --> 00:42:07.750
You also have to know are your backups working?

00:42:07.750 --> 00:42:12.670
I can't tell you how many times I've worked
with folks that they thought they had a back

00:42:12.670 --> 00:42:14.380
up but they really didn't.

00:42:14.380 --> 00:42:16.800
And that's a really painful thing.

00:42:16.800 --> 00:42:22.280
And I put a link on here for this American
Cell Biology article that I wrote about backups,

00:42:22.280 --> 00:42:27.290
that has links to a whole bunch of-- you can
read about all the disaster stories and things

00:42:27.290 --> 00:42:32.990
that happen when you don't manage your data
and keep track of things.

00:42:32.990 --> 00:42:37.820
And the funding agencies require this, even
though it's in some sense not to the level

00:42:37.820 --> 00:42:42.970
of detail that you need to run your experiments
or to run your computational pipeline.

00:42:42.970 --> 00:42:47.420
But it's an important thing to think about.

00:42:47.420 --> 00:42:51.510
Other best practices that are also really
useful are things like version control.

00:42:51.510 --> 00:42:56.780
So especially for a collaborative software
development and group software development,

00:42:56.780 --> 00:43:02.930
is to have some way to manage all the changes
to the documents and share concurrent access

00:43:02.930 --> 00:43:06.760
with these documents with other people.

00:43:06.760 --> 00:43:11.350
I've talked a little bit about benchmarking
and documentation.

00:43:11.350 --> 00:43:19.790
But troubleshooting again is-- I keep on saying
everything is the most important thing so

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:20.790
it's rough here.

00:43:20.790 --> 00:43:23.500
But I'll tell you, this troubleshooting--
so I include this xkcd cartoon.

00:43:23.500 --> 00:43:33.900
The important thing here is that we don't
magically know how to do everything.

00:43:33.900 --> 00:43:41.640
And it's trying to come up with a way to be
self-reliant and find ways to solve your problems.

00:43:41.640 --> 00:43:43.360
And it's not that other people aren't around
to help.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:44.360
They are.

00:43:44.360 --> 00:43:49.550
But it's a really powerful tool when you can
troubleshoot and move your science together

00:43:49.550 --> 00:43:50.550
by yourself.

00:43:50.550 --> 00:43:54.370
And in fact on here, Google is somewhere down
this flowchart.

00:43:54.370 --> 00:43:58.320
But it's almost like
Google's at the start now because that's the

00:43:58.320 --> 00:44:00.600
best way to do it.

00:44:00.600 --> 00:44:03.420
And again, of course things go wrong.

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:07.620
And troubleshooting really is something that
people can learn and really get good at.

00:44:07.620 --> 00:44:11.350
And it's this logical systematic search for
the source of the problem.

00:44:11.350 --> 00:44:15.920
And I think sometimes it's almost the most
complicated thing, is that often there's more

00:44:15.920 --> 00:44:18.070
than one thing that's wrong at a time.

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:24.620
And so that makes it more complex to help
sort out what the problem might be.

00:44:24.620 --> 00:44:29.480
But you have to do this.

00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:33.610
No matter how you use computers you have to
do this.

00:44:33.610 --> 00:44:38.540
So some other approaches-- and again, I'm
mentioning these approaches just to raise

00:44:38.540 --> 00:44:40.540
awareness.

00:44:40.540 --> 00:44:44.340
And again, this goes back to whatever-- it
depends on what you're doing.

00:44:44.340 --> 00:44:46.000
That's the important part.

00:44:46.000 --> 00:44:50.940
What you're doing might be totally fine using
Excel or R on your laptop.

00:44:50.940 --> 00:44:55.230
And one of the most powerful things about
that is that you can port your code to other

00:44:55.230 --> 00:45:00.000
environments and scale up to larger data sets,
so you can start locally and start easily

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:05.950
and then move to larger resources if you need
them later.

00:45:05.950 --> 00:45:09.880
And so there are high throughput computing
resources.

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:16.240
So sometimes we might call this embarrassingly
parallel, where you have lots of independent

00:45:16.240 --> 00:45:21.800
tasks that might take a long time to run,
and they can be used so you probably heard

00:45:21.800 --> 00:45:23.970
of setting it home or folding it home.

00:45:23.970 --> 00:45:27.770
High performance computing is more around
using these tightly coupled resources to solve

00:45:27.770 --> 00:45:31.710
a single large problem.

00:45:31.710 --> 00:45:36.680
Graphics processing units-- again, that's
another tool where these are processors that

00:45:36.680 --> 00:45:41.270
are really good at doing rendering images
and they're optimized for doing those kind

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:42.270
of mathematics.

00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:45.270
They happen to be good at machine learning
and some other things.

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:46.730
They're not general purpose.

00:45:46.730 --> 00:45:50.510
They're not good for everything, but they
can be a really good tool depending on what

00:45:50.510 --> 00:45:52.220
you want to do.

00:45:52.220 --> 00:45:59.350
Cloud computing-- that's another pool of resources
where you'll get a virtual machine, and so

00:45:59.350 --> 00:46:05.570
you kind of almost get your own version of
the operating system that you are in control

00:46:05.570 --> 00:46:09.700
of and you can do things with.

00:46:09.700 --> 00:46:12.600
But you're sharing the physical machine with
other users.

00:46:12.600 --> 00:46:16.250
With high performance computing, you'll get
the whole node.

00:46:16.250 --> 00:46:17.250
You'll get all the memory.

00:46:17.250 --> 00:46:18.250
You'll get all the storage.

00:46:18.250 --> 00:46:19.450
You'll get all the bandwidth.

00:46:19.450 --> 00:46:24.650
It's dedicated to you because normally when
you're processing needs all of that-- all

00:46:24.650 --> 00:46:25.710
of those resources.

00:46:25.710 --> 00:46:30.610
But there's a whole variety of computing that's
needed an application.

00:46:30.610 --> 00:46:32.850
So again, it's just trying to find the right
thing.

00:46:32.850 --> 00:46:36.290
Of course, you've probably heard of Hadoop
and a lot of the big data stuff again for

00:46:36.290 --> 00:46:40.090
large scale data analytics.

00:46:40.090 --> 00:46:45.610
There's lots of national computing resources
that are available to you.

00:46:45.610 --> 00:46:50.410
So you could do a prototype on your laptop
or somewhere else and then scale up.

00:46:50.410 --> 00:46:53.620
You may have local campus computing resources.

00:46:53.620 --> 00:47:02.030
NIH Commons-- NIH has some computational resources
and data resources that are available or are

00:47:02.030 --> 00:47:03.450
being made
available.

00:47:03.450 --> 00:47:08.090
And there's going to be a talk on this in
a couple sessions by Vivien Bonazzi, so that's

00:47:08.090 --> 00:47:09.770
going to be a good talk.

00:47:09.770 --> 00:47:18.290
I wanted to mention NSF has this Excede Environment,
where they have supercomputing cycles that

00:47:18.290 --> 00:47:20.240
you can apply to use.

00:47:20.240 --> 00:47:21.680
They have cloud computing environments.

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:22.680
They have GPUs.

00:47:22.680 --> 00:47:24.330
They have lots of stuff.

00:47:24.330 --> 00:47:28.740
The Department of Energy also has leadership
computing projects.

00:47:28.740 --> 00:47:30.210
That's for large scale research.

00:47:30.210 --> 00:47:34.430
I mentioned Amazon and all and of course,
there's lots of other cloud services.

00:47:34.430 --> 00:47:37.970
I mentioned VMs a little bit.

00:47:37.970 --> 00:47:42.170
Containers are also a really popular thing
now, were you kind of have a complete contained

00:47:42.170 --> 00:47:45.150
environment for your system tools so it makes
it very portable.

00:47:45.150 --> 00:47:48.340
And a lot of people like this for reproducibility.

00:47:48.340 --> 00:47:53.700
I wanted to mention there is the Science Gateways
and lots of other tools that you can get cycles

00:47:53.700 --> 00:47:56.230
and someone else is maintaining the software.

00:47:56.230 --> 00:48:01.760
So they can be really powerful and easy way
to use tools.

00:48:01.760 --> 00:48:08.170
Machine learning, natural language processing--
machine learning around helping to get the

00:48:08.170 --> 00:48:11.570
computer to adapt to your data and make predictions
on it.

00:48:11.570 --> 00:48:20.710
Natural language processing-- being able to
interpret human language in some way.

00:48:20.710 --> 00:48:24.160
I'm trying to go through all this because
I have a bunch of other stuff I'd like to

00:48:24.160 --> 00:48:25.160
say.

00:48:25.160 --> 00:48:31.790
So this slide is just to show you just how
complex the environment is.

00:48:31.790 --> 00:48:36.180
And so again, you really have to know what
you want to do and then you can help figure

00:48:36.180 --> 00:48:39.170
out what the right tools are for you to use
it.

00:48:39.170 --> 00:48:44.150
So now putting all these approaches into the
biomedical context and then also comparing

00:48:44.150 --> 00:48:50.050
it against this evolving computing landscape--
so this slide is meant to give you some idea

00:48:50.050 --> 00:48:56.140
of the breadth and depth of research and different
approaches and resources that are available.

00:48:56.140 --> 00:49:01.240
So you can see at the top here some of the
different research targets.

00:49:01.240 --> 00:49:04.000
And AMIA is the American Medical Informatics
Association.

00:49:04.000 --> 00:49:10.530
So these are their categories for research
subspecialties and then you can see this happens

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:12.790
to be a Sinai example.

00:49:12.790 --> 00:49:19.700
But you can see how there is a whole span
of everything from population health and bioinformatics

00:49:19.700 --> 00:49:23.570
and all of this spans these different computing
domains.

00:49:23.570 --> 00:49:25.670
And then you can see the different approaches
on the bottom.

00:49:25.670 --> 00:49:31.540
I've been talking about a lot of computational
science approaches and data science approaches.

00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:35.990
And there's national resources-- national
data resources-- and lots of things on the

00:49:35.990 --> 00:49:36.990
site.

00:49:36.990 --> 00:49:42.100
So this hopefully gives you some context and
idea about the breadth and some of the approaches

00:49:42.100 --> 00:49:44.220
that can be used.

00:49:44.220 --> 00:49:55.740
This slide shows you in some sense how again,
all different kinds of data, and how complex

00:49:55.740 --> 00:50:02.280
the problem is to be able to analyze this
data and derive meaning from it, which is

00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:03.440
the whole point of the big data.

00:50:03.440 --> 00:50:06.340
It's a knowledge initiative.

00:50:06.340 --> 00:50:12.320
I wanted to contrast all this with-- over
time now, the processors-- here's a graph

00:50:12.320 --> 00:50:16.190
showing microprocessor trend
data.

00:50:16.190 --> 00:50:20.340
And the corner frequency is no longer increasing.

00:50:20.340 --> 00:50:25.860
And so in some sense, a lot of the performance
stuff that I've been talking about-- it becomes

00:50:25.860 --> 00:50:31.440
even more important because being able to
leverage those multiple causes that are on

00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:38.240
your chips and the whole concept of parallelism
is the only path to increasing performance

00:50:38.240 --> 00:50:40.030
now.

00:50:40.030 --> 00:50:45.410
And this next slide came from the National
Academies report about this, and about how

00:50:45.410 --> 00:50:46.480
important this is.

00:50:46.480 --> 00:50:56.370
Because if your code stays serial and you
stay with, maybe I'll say a naive approach,

00:50:56.370 --> 00:51:00.760
you will just be left behind and your code
and everything will be slower by the amount

00:51:00.760 --> 00:51:02.790
of factor that this is moving forward.

00:51:02.790 --> 00:51:09.280
So you can see how quickly this is changing.

00:51:09.280 --> 00:51:14.360
And there were two recent reports, I just
want to say, that talk about a lot of this

00:51:14.360 --> 00:51:16.200
in detail.

00:51:16.200 --> 00:51:21.230
And at the same time, there's the Precision
Medicine Initiative that's out, where a million

00:51:21.230 --> 00:51:23.420
volunteers are going to share their genetic
data.

00:51:23.420 --> 00:51:27.740
And so I look-- what are the computational
requirements of data requirements that are

00:51:27.740 --> 00:51:29.990
going to fall out of just that one initiative.

00:51:29.990 --> 00:51:35.060
And the VA has a similar initiative with a
million patients as well.

00:51:35.060 --> 00:51:39.890
And so you can see here, we're talking hundreds
of petabytes of data-- of raw input data.

00:51:39.890 --> 00:51:43.440
That doesn't count all the analysis of the
data.

00:51:43.440 --> 00:51:46.260
And then you look at the images.

00:51:46.260 --> 00:51:48.680
You're thinking about a million patients.

00:51:48.680 --> 00:51:50.600
You have an exabyte of data in terms of images.

00:51:50.600 --> 00:51:56.430
And again, an exabyte is 1,000 terabytes and
a terabyte is 1,000 gigabytes and so on.

00:51:56.430 --> 00:52:03.640
So you can start to get some scale of how
this is a complicated problem, both from the

00:52:03.640 --> 00:52:08.640
size of it but also from the complexity of
it, especially like you saw in the structure

00:52:08.640 --> 00:52:09.790
and unstructured data.

00:52:09.790 --> 00:52:15.210
How can we correlate all these data sets so
that we can derive some knowledge from it?

00:52:15.210 --> 00:52:23.280
And so in part, to help to address the problem
of the processing speeds not getting faster,

00:52:23.280 --> 00:52:29.830
there's a national strategic computing initiative
that's going on now it's between NSF, DOE,

00:52:29.830 --> 00:52:30.830
NIH.

00:52:30.830 --> 00:52:35.470
And this is to help develop the next generation
computer that's going to be two orders of

00:52:35.470 --> 00:52:40.190
magnitude faster than the machines we have
now.

00:52:40.190 --> 00:52:44.950
And this initiative obviously is important
to all of the things that I've been talking

00:52:44.950 --> 00:52:56.480
about, including the cancer moonshot initiative,
which there's already some initial projects

00:52:56.480 --> 00:52:59.940
that are going on-- some pilot projects--
between the Department of Energy and the National

00:52:59.940 --> 00:53:06.350
Cancer Institute to help work on these problems
together.

00:53:06.350 --> 00:53:11.960
And you can see some examples of the problems
here-- some molecular dynamic simulations,

00:53:11.960 --> 00:53:16.430
things to do with medical records, lots of
things that are bringing some of this deep

00:53:16.430 --> 00:53:23.000
learning, machine learning, all of these kind
of technologies to bear on these problems.

00:53:23.000 --> 00:53:30.700
And I just wanted to point out even in the
Cancer Moonshot Blue Ribbon Panel Report,

00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:36.910
four of their recommendations have to do with
that are informatics and computationally related.

00:53:36.910 --> 00:53:41.820
So you can see how all of these technologies
are really important.

00:53:41.820 --> 00:53:48.550
And so big data and computer-- necessary for
improved health care.

00:53:48.550 --> 00:53:54.730
And there's lots of exciting opportunities
here, but of course there are serious challenges

00:53:54.730 --> 00:53:58.970
of how are we going to do this.

00:53:58.970 --> 00:54:06.320
And some of it is-- even basic things like
understanding each other between the different

00:54:06.320 --> 00:54:08.820
domains, because we all speak different languages.

00:54:08.820 --> 00:54:11.920
And that's an important thing to.

00:54:11.920 --> 00:54:17.000
And code lasts forever as you see here.

00:54:17.000 --> 00:54:22.090
Developing the workforce-- and so that's why
to me, this seminar series is so important

00:54:22.090 --> 00:54:26.520
because there's just simply not enough people
to help work on these problems.

00:54:26.520 --> 00:54:34.920
And it's just that we can't move forward without
having enough people and enough people in

00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:37.620
ingenuity to work on this.

00:54:37.620 --> 00:54:41.150
And I wanted to mention here are some resources.

00:54:41.150 --> 00:54:47.090
I'll point out the NIH erudite, which is a
really clever, interesting resource.

00:54:47.090 --> 00:54:51.270
It can help you find out about other training
opportunities and other things.

00:54:51.270 --> 00:54:58.290
But there's all of these links on here-- have
really good data there.

00:54:58.290 --> 00:55:06.890
And I just wanted to do a quick recap here
about basically how to be productive with

00:55:06.890 --> 00:55:08.560
scientific computing.

00:55:08.560 --> 00:55:10.620
Make sure you're using the right tool.

00:55:10.620 --> 00:55:15.650
Make sure you're validating your system--
thinking about it.

00:55:15.650 --> 00:55:17.200
Because your time isn't free.

00:55:17.200 --> 00:55:20.200
The computing isn't free.

00:55:20.200 --> 00:55:22.280
What's the best way to use these resources?

00:55:22.280 --> 00:55:26.480
Thinking about the sustainability for the
future, and some of that's around who's going

00:55:26.480 --> 00:55:29.450
to take over your pipeline or reuse it, maintain
it.

00:55:29.450 --> 00:55:38.540
Some of its surrounds if you're software is
going to last a long time, does it effectively

00:55:38.540 --> 00:55:42.880
make use of the resources and the cores and
everything else that exists now?

00:55:42.880 --> 00:55:49.160
Troubleshooting, documenting, disseminating,
your results and your data, and really working

00:55:49.160 --> 00:55:52.060
with researchers from outside your area to
help work on this.

00:55:52.060 --> 00:55:55.730
So the more efficient your computing is, the
more science you can get done.

00:55:55.730 --> 00:55:59.540
Lots of people have helped me.

00:55:59.540 --> 00:56:06.820
And I apologize to anyone I don't have on
this list, but it really is a village to have

00:56:06.820 --> 00:56:09.870
an effective computing environment.

00:56:09.870 --> 00:56:15.120
Last thing I want to say is, these happy faces
are from a summer school that we had that

00:56:15.120 --> 00:56:21.460
was funded by NIH PD2K, this community research
education engagement for data science.

00:56:21.460 --> 00:56:24.000
This was the computational genomics summer
school.

00:56:24.000 --> 00:56:25.820
It's two weeks in the summer in New York City.

00:56:25.820 --> 00:56:26.820
We go to Columbia.

00:56:26.820 --> 00:56:28.180
We go to NYU.

00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:31.090
We go to New York Genome Center or Weill Cornell.

00:56:31.090 --> 00:56:35.600
We have lots of different projects and we
teach a little bit about scientific computing,

00:56:35.600 --> 00:56:40.320
a little bit about genomics, running the genomics
pipeline.

00:56:40.320 --> 00:56:45.710
And it's open to everyone nationally.

00:56:45.710 --> 00:56:49.310
Please apply if you'd like to come.

00:56:49.310 --> 00:56:52.411
And we have a Masters of Science in biomedical
informatics.

00:56:52.411 --> 00:56:58.600
And we're trying to help grow the workforce
and the next generation of folks to help us

00:56:58.600 --> 00:57:00.040
work on all these problems.

00:57:00.040 --> 00:57:01.890
So thank you very much.

00:57:01.890 --> 00:57:06.240
Thank you, Dr. Kovatch, for this wonderful
talk.

00:57:06.240 --> 00:57:08.480
So we have time for one question.

00:57:08.480 --> 00:57:14.940
You've given an overview of the
different computing environments and frameworks

00:57:14.940 --> 00:57:19.610
that we're seeing both in biomedical big data
and data science.

00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:25.190
A lot of institutions have been previously
and are still continuing to develop their

00:57:25.190 --> 00:57:31.570
own high performance computing frameworks
on campus, whereas we're also obviously seeing

00:57:31.570 --> 00:57:38.250
a lot of interest from being able to move
to cloud computing through things like Amazon,

00:57:38.250 --> 00:57:40.340
Microsoft Azure and such.

00:57:40.340 --> 00:57:45.080
Do you have any words of wisdom in terms of
where to draw the line between which one to

00:57:45.080 --> 00:57:46.080
use?

00:57:46.080 --> 00:57:47.080
I do.

00:57:47.080 --> 00:57:48.080
I do.

00:57:48.080 --> 00:57:51.470
This is an excellent question.

00:57:51.470 --> 00:57:55.760
I guess maybe the first thing I would say
is that depends on what you want to do.

00:57:55.760 --> 00:57:59.800
I would say the reason why-- at least I'll
speak from the Sinai perspective-- the reason

00:57:59.800 --> 00:58:05.010
why we still have computational resources
on campus is because we can do with a lot

00:58:05.010 --> 00:58:09.600
more-- I will say there's a cost reason.

00:58:09.600 --> 00:58:14.101
For us to do all of the same computing that
we did last year at Mt.

00:58:14.101 --> 00:58:19.300
Sinai to do it on Amazon, it would have been
$12 million and we did it for a tiny fraction

00:58:19.300 --> 00:58:20.630
of that.

00:58:20.630 --> 00:58:25.510
And so I think some of it can be-- if you
have the right kind of scale and you have

00:58:25.510 --> 00:58:30.480
enough of a certain kind of computing, then
it may make sense to do things locally.

00:58:30.480 --> 00:58:35.860
But if you have a web page or a small database
or whatever, it might make sense to do it

00:58:35.860 --> 00:58:36.860
in the cloud.

00:58:36.860 --> 00:58:41.310
Or if you don't have the local infrastructure
or-- it just depends.

00:58:41.310 --> 00:58:48.670
And I think each institution has to make their
own-- makes their own choices.

00:58:48.670 --> 00:58:53.430
And again, it has to be tied back to what
you actually need and what makes sense.

00:58:53.430 --> 00:58:56.030
And you have to look at the different trade
offs.

00:58:56.030 --> 00:59:01.090
In my talk here there's different papers,
like this Magellan paper that was funded by

00:59:01.090 --> 00:59:07.540
the Department of Energy done at [INAUDIBLE]
where they did a lot of comparisons and applications

00:59:07.540 --> 00:59:09.540
and all the different trade offs.

00:59:09.540 --> 00:59:14.580
So I'll just say it totally depends on what
you're doing-- the scale out computing.

00:59:14.580 --> 00:59:18.590
And also I'll just say that National Academy
report, the one that was just done last year,

00:59:18.590 --> 00:59:23.220
addresses this specifically about when is
the right time to use cloud and again what's

00:59:23.220 --> 00:59:24.220
the trade off?

00:59:24.220 --> 00:59:31.340
So I think it totally depends and I think
there's lots of good resources.

00:59:31.340 --> 00:59:32.340
But it totally depends.

00:59:32.340 --> 00:59:39.020
And even cloud resources within NSF computing,
Excede and all this stuff-- there's cloud

00:59:39.020 --> 00:59:40.160
resources there too.

00:59:40.160 --> 00:59:43.530
So it just depends.

00:59:43.530 --> 00:59:44.580
OK.

00:59:44.580 --> 00:59:49.040
Thank you so much and we'll close it for now.

00:59:49.040 --> 00:59:50.040
Thank you.

00:59:50.040 --> 00:59:51.040
OK.

00:59:51.040 --> 00:59:51.042
Thank you.

